{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794149247,"nonce":"3nQumR6HGxw7Q/l01KpKNy4NvFc=","message":"iPhone 12 Pro Max, 2026-11-08, mobile/example, same scene and CPU work, one variable at a time (temporary environment switches, removed before commit):\n- Scheduler thread: main-thread ticking or the background thread, 29.1 fps either way. Not the limit.\n- Fragment shader replaced by a flat fill for the core and shadow SDF pipelines: 60 fps (display-locked). Flat for the shadow pipeline alone: 41 to 46 fps. Flat for core alone: 60 fps. So nearly all of the gap is the core `fs_main`.\n- Early return inside the core `fs_main`: every stage up to and including the SDF shape distance and anti-aliasing ran at 60 fps (cool device). The cost starts after it, in the fill colour (gradient table loop), border, shadow compositing, mask and CSS filter code, which most primitives never use. That points at register pressure and occupancy of the uber-shader (the compiler provisions for every feature on every fragment), not at the SDF maths.\n- Caveat: the A14 throttles within a few runs, so absolute fps drifts from 29 down to 12 on the same variant; compare only runs close in time, with a cool-down.\n\nWhy the SDF is evaluated every frame: the renderer redraws every pixel each frame and evaluates the signed distance analytically in the fragment shader for anti-aliased edges; on mobile there is no retained layer or static cache (the desktop compositor has one), so unchanged content is re-shaded too. During a scroll everything moves, so even desktop-style caching would need the scroll content as a texture to translate.\n\nNext: specialise the core pipeline by primitive features (a simple variant for solid fill, plain or uniform border, circular corners, no mask or filter, chosen per run at batch time; `override` constants or a preprocessor keep one source), then a retained scroll layer.","files":null}]}